# tools/al/ales_graft_witness.rish — the graft, a selected span copied or moved onto a second point (ALES24).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES24.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-fill-ales24-lotus-graft.md
# Stands on: lotus/selection.rye (ALES23) — the validated Selection this rung relocates; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — splice and cut,
# applied to each channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip whose two channels share one length.
#
# lotus/graft.rye copies and moves a selected span onto a second point. The crux: a grafted span lands byte-for-byte at the
# destination, keeps the two channels in LOCKSTEP, and never corrupts itself when source and destination overlap — both channels'
# span samples are snapshotted into a bounded scratch BEFORE any splice or cut, the same (at, span) passed to each channel with
# capacity checked first, and a move re-bases the destination for the samples the cut removed so the net length is unchanged. The
# selftest proves: a copy duplicates the span in both channels with the source untouched; a copy before its own source still reads
# the original span (the self-overlap proof); a move relocates the span with the net length unchanged and re-bases correctly; a move
# onto its own edge is the identity; every edge refuses by name (BadDest / ClipFull). Purely local — no network, no keys, no funds.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_graft_witness.rish

say "ales-graft: Language EN — ALES24 lets a keeper copy or move a marked span onto a second point: double the chorus, relocate the verse, lift the count-in off the front and drop it after the bridge, across both channels at once."
say "ales-graft: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: both channels' span samples are snapshotted before any edit, so a copy reads the original audio even when its destination overlaps its source; the same (at, span) is passed to each channel, so a copy grows both equally and a move keeps both equal, the graft atomic and the stereo always aligned."
say "ales-graft: Lens TAME — timeline.splice/cut and selection.cut reused whole (no new edit, no new bound); the snapshot bounded by max_clip; the destination and capacity checked before either channel is touched so a bad graft refuses by name; siloed to lotus/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no meter, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/graft"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/graft.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-graft: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/graft.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-graft: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-graft: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-graft' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-graft: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-graft line"

say "ales-graft: GREEN — a keeper copies and moves a marked span as one across the stereo image: the graft snapshots before it mutates, so an overlapping copy reads the original audio; the same span lands in both channels in lockstep; a move re-bases its destination and keeps the net length unchanged. The next Lotus rung can offer the choice of law where a crossfade meets a grafted edit, a named clipboard so a span survives across masters, or carry a grafted span across the ALES0 audio wire as its own framed payload."
